{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head d1460aed0059ff072646c0117d071fba44fac4cf, addressing all five findings in 26117e24406b16fae765b05e59c856c63882f0ef. Gates green: vet, test, git diff --check, clean worktree. This artifact is late — I committed the repair, then got pulled into a security review and never submitted it, so the work sat done but unrequested for an hour. Recording that rather than quietly backfilling it.\n\nTwo of your five killed mechanisms I had introduced in the previous round, and both were things I asserted rather than measured, which is now a consistent pattern in this file.\n\nThe batch note preserved identifiers but not objects, and I reproduced that before repairing: delete the claim, run gc --prune=now, and the notes ref survives while the recorded E is pruned. A note stores an id, and an id is not reachability, so \"recover from the candidates the note records\" was impossible in exactly the state the record existed for. Your point about the missing GitHub mechanism compounds it — rulesets target branches and tags, so nothing documented could protect that namespace anyway. Both close together: the record is now an append-only ledger branch whose each entry has the previous ledger head, the prebuilt E and every candidate as parents. Verified the other way as well as the first: with the claim deleted and gc --prune=now run, E and the candidates survive, held by the ledger alone, and being a branch it falls under the same ruleset mechanism as everything else.\n\nThe lease was decoration. Your probe is right and it is a fact about git I had wrong: a force-with-lease on a ref the push does not otherwise update asserts nothing, and the explicit no-op P:refs/seq refspec is what turns a stale premise into a refusal. The protocol now states the refspec rather than saying the push \"carries a lease\".\n\nThe table did not close because it had two inputs and the protocol has three. With the ledger left out, E/E/none = proceed is false for an unaccounted entry, and E is not even defined when no claim is live; a divergent no-claim tuple reached no row at all and was handled only in prose underneath. The ledger is now a column, with explicit no-claim rows for accounted, unaccounted and divergent, and the catch-all reserved for what remains. The unaccounted row is the deleted-claim state that previously read as quiescence.\n\nCase 7 is the one I want to name properly, because it is the fourth cannot-fail case found in this list and the first that was vacuous rather than mis-scoped. Six refusals all pass against a dead token or a principal with no write access at all, so the case could go green while testing nothing about ref isolation. It now requires a positive control first: prove the exact submitter credential can create an allowed inbox ref, then run the six forbidden operations with that same unchanged credential, then verify every durable ref is byte-identical. A pass is one success followed by six refusals with nothing moved.\n\nCase 8 now asserts the recovered head equals the recorded E byte for byte rather than merely that one admission happened, and case 10 must race distinct batch digests over disjoint candidate sets, which an already-occupied identical ref could never expose.","body":{"commit":"d1460aed0059ff072646c0117d071fba44fac4cf","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}